WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.940
Hi, everyone. Welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners, episode 106 for July 18, 2026.

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:12.480
Let's break down today's coolest AI news so anyone can understand it. Let's go.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:18.880
You know how a single unexpected text can derail an entire group chat and send everyone off

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:24.760
in a new direction. That everyday experience is the key to understanding today's biggest AI

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:31.460
security story. Researchers have found a way to stop AI agents from carrying out harmful tasks

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:36.000
by slipping hidden shutdown instructions into the information those agents read.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:42.540
The technique is called context bombing, and it works because the attacking AI treats every

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:49.400
bit of texted C's as equally important. Think of an AI agent as a very literal assistant who follows

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:54.900
a long-to-do list line-by-line without ever questioning whether one item belongs there.

00:00:55.460 --> 00:01:00.820
If you hide a new line that says stop everything and go home, the assistant reads it as part of

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:07.660
its normal job and simply quits. This matters because AI agents are already appearing in tools that

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:13.720
scan code, answer emails, or manage accounts, and the same agents could be misused by people who

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:20.040
want to steal data. The research shows that simple text tricks can act like invisible safety rails,

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:24.720
giving regular users better protection without needing any technical setup themselves.

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:30.880
For you it means the websites and apps you rely on every day could soon include these quiet

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:37.360
defenses against automated attacks. The idea also reminds us that powerful tools need thoughtful

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:43.440
limits and the limits can sometimes be surprisingly straightforward. Right now there is no public

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:49.420
demo to try, but the research gives us a clear window into how future security features might quietly

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:55.260
guard the tools we use. Okay, now for the part of the show where we really look under the hood.

00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:01.100
Let's walk through exactly how context bombing fools an AI agent step by step.

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:07.320
Imagine you are scrolling through a long group chat on your phone and each new message changes what

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:13.660
the conversation is about. An AI agent works the same way, moving through its instructions one line

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:20.360
at a time, and deciding its next action based on everything it has read so far. The trick is to plant

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:26.920
a new instruction inside normal looking content, something like ignore everything else and stop working,

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:32.620
because the agent has no separate safety checker that reviews whether the new line is trustworthy.

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:37.940
It simply treats the planted message as part of its regular task list and follows it.

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:44.080
The whole process only succeeds because today's agents are built to give equal weight to every piece

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:49.800
of text they receive. Once you picture the agent as someone who keeps reading and acting without

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:54.900
pausing to double check the source, the method suddenly feels logical rather than mysterious.

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:58.660
That simple picture is what makes the whole idea click.

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:05.100
Now let's look at two chat bots you can try right this second and see which one matches the way you

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:11.640
like to learn. Chat GPT and Gemini are both free and available on your phone or laptop.

00:03:12.380 --> 00:03:17.300
One might feel more like a creative storyteller while the other feels stronger at breaking down

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:23.800
homework into clear steps. Open both at the same time and ask them the exact same question about

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:28.600
something you care about, such as explaining photosynthesis like you are in middle school.

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:34.540
Notice which answer feels clearer or more fun to read, then try a second question like

00:03:34.540 --> 00:03:40.860
three ideas for a short video about climate change. After a few exchanges you will start to sense which

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:46.780
chatbot fits your own thinking style better. The experiment takes just a few minutes and costs nothing,

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:52.060
yet it shows you how different AI tools can feel even when they answer the same prompt.

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:57.940
Here is another quick experiment that connects to the same idea of learning how AI behaves.

00:03:58.540 --> 00:04:04.880
Pick a topic you are studying right now for school or a hobby and ask both chat bots to give you a short

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:11.280
summary plus three follow-up questions. Watch how each one chooses which details to highlight and

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:17.280
how the follow-up questions steer the conversation. You will quickly see that small differences in wording

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:23.480
and tone can make one feel more helpful than the other for your particular goal. Before we wrap up,

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:29.480
here are two smaller stories worth knowing. Zooks is recalling some of its self-driving software.

